PianoWifi is new code for UniXNU-piano (BSD-2-Clause-Patent, LICENSE). Levels 1 (read-only survey), 2 (power), 3 (the PCIe0 link)
and 4 (the firmware over MHI). What it takes from other work, and from where:

UniXNU-sheng (unixnu-research/UniXNU-sheng, main 00ba90984e831e4c6ce30ec3d8a56e6434e9f1af), BSD-2-Clause-Patent (LICENSE sha256
5b1d3d911103cb0f41641ec0964c5ac1f7e229f57aa072b6ac38bae913a1307b; SM8550 + WCN7850, the Wi-Fi path hardware-verified there per its
porting.toml), package/WifiProbe.cpp (sha256 72f46e8229865eff68feae9a3002b919ad3b610e634d93877879f4960d5b01a0):
  Design only, re-implemented (no code copied): a level that only looks before any level powers anything; the controller's
  registers read only when its power domain is on and its interface clocks run ("a read of an unclocked block stalls the bus");
  the TLMM pin layout (also used by PianoPower and PianoDwc3); the SMPS peripheral page rule 0x9b + 3 * (n - 1). Its SM8550
  addresses (DBI 0x60000000, GCC branch offsets, the v6 PHY layout) are NOT piano's and are not used.

UniXNU-Pagani-Drivers (github.com/Shad0w23333/UniXNU-Pagani-Drivers 3d570b7653bf9831def8fe49b339b42990508d83, also
IssacL04/UniXNU-Pagani-Drivers), drivers/PaganiWifi, BSD-2-Clause-Patent (LICENSE sha256 as UniXNU-sheng's; SM8750 + the same
17cb:110e chip on the OnePlus 13T; host-tested only, never run on a device), PaganiWifiTables.h (sha256
001f808ba5dd4356b1e31efcfd9631c0a1064d71201c29f73cf55f3efb859516), WifiBringup.cpp (sha256
bb40a62361e06870348cd66eecca0634fb0e48d36a60afcb3f3acb0035ab3cd2):
  Facts only, cross-checked against mainline Linux (below) where mainline has them: the SM8750 PCIe0 GDSC, collapse vote, branch
  CBCR / vote bit / hardware-gating table, BCRs, RCGs and pipe mux; the v7 PHY layout; the LDO page rule 0xc0 + n on SM8750's
  PMICs (its running DT's OCP notifier names); the PMK8550 gpio3 sleep clock pin; gpio19 / 202 / 203; the cmd-db names of the
  supplies and the PCIe NoC BCM (SN4); the PARF offsets of the SM8550 programming model, the BDF-to-SID hash rule (CRC-8,
  polynomial 7, high byte first) and the chip's BAR0 remap window register (0x3278 for 0x110e). Board values of the 13T (its rails,
  PPIDs, pin states) are not piano's and are not used: every rail, voltage range and pin of levels 2 and 3 comes from piano's own
  boot DT.
  Also taken, with its provenance question recorded in WifiPhySeq.h and docs/drivers/wifi.md section 10.4:
    PaganiWifiTables.h phy::kSequence (the 128 writes of the QMP v7 PHY initialisation, which PaganiWifi read out of the 13T's
    running device tree property qcom,phy-sequence) -> WifiPhySeq.h kPhySequence, the same offsets and values in the same order.
    It is not mainline's GPL table (it differs in five writes, which PaganiWifi lists), it reached this repository through
    BSD-licensed code, and each entry is a register value; whether a vendor DT sequence and Qualcomm's own driver tables are
    "independent" cannot be settled from here, and the alternative (reading the sequence from piano's own stock DT at run time) is
    written down in wifi.md.
  Design re-expressed, no code copied (the structures and names are ours; the steps and their order are theirs):
    WifiBringup.cpp powerOn / linkUp / endpoint (sha256 bb40a62361e06870348cd66eecca0634fb0e48d36a60afcb3f3acb0035ab3cd2)
      -> WifiBringup.h powerUp / linkUp: the order rails -> WLAN_EN -> GDSC -> clocks -> resets -> PARF -> PHY -> BDF-to-SID ->
      root port -> PERST# -> LTSSM -> L0 -> configuration window -> memory window -> BAR0, the "a vote only adds" rule for the
      rails, the pin-write order (level, configuration, level again, from sheng's setPin), PERST# low for at least 100 ms, and the
      idea of a portable sequence over callbacks so that a host model checks the order and the values.
    MhiBoot.c / MhiBoot.h (sha256 32b78ccb4e127b52296d34f4be4b431b9d311c105296b66c799d6d247b653b92,
      0a5bbf7c07cd2022e2e5e8e219526540a009d37743ddd381936169baf63634e6; themselves ported from UniXNU-sheng package/MhiBoot.c,
      sha256 9743ba00f26520b9322eba71ff3721d77216e3882dedbec23757d20b645f3c67) -> WifiMhi.h: the BHI/BHIe phase machine (PBL check,
      reset on a system error, the image doorbell, READY, the contexts, M0, the vector table, mission mode), the firmware file's
      split rule (a second ELF at the boot loader's size), and the register and bit facts.
    WifiMhiLoad.c (sha256 947df29b8f0cf3c158f528dae86ab8e58abcca631627fe2133a4bd72fb65d15d) -> WifiMhi.h mhiContextsPlan /
      mhiContextsProgram / mhiEventAck: 128 channel contexts left disabled, two event rings (32 on vector 1, 256 on vector 2), the
      primary command ring, the host address range 0..2^36-1, and giving the device its contexts both before the boot loader starts
      and again after it resets the registers.
    WifiDma.c / WifiDma.h (sha256 6e7f0311e724d424723c7edc0117fdb85c7ba41931b42eb0a2fef1aad57c2115) -> WifiMhi.h Arena: one
      contiguous window, every buffer aligned to its own size rounded up to a power of two (sheng saw the boot loader refuse an
      image buffer that was only page aligned). Ours is a bump allocator, not their first-fit list with merging: level 4 never
      frees a buffer.
    PaganiWifi.cpp dmaWindow / cleanToPoc / chipAt (part of sha256 of the file in that tree) -> PianoWifi.cpp dmaWindow,
      cleanToPoc, chipRead: a 36-bit contiguous buffer whose cacheable alias is cleaned once and then only reached through a
      write-combined mapping, and the chip's register window arithmetic.

